// @run
// @log 7
// @log 8
// @warning Unused variable `wv` (prefix with `_` to suppress, or remove it)
// @warning Unused variable `wv` (prefix with `_` to suppress, or remove it)
// @hint redundant type annotation: `wv` is inferred as `{g: {[string]: Dot}}` — remove it to lean on inference
// The `GW` annotation is the LOAD-BEARING half of the witness and the hint is declared, not
// obeyed: inference gives `{g: {[string]: Dot}}`, and it is precisely the disagreement between
// that and the annotated `{g: {[string]: Circle}}` — two layout twins, two mv slots, one
// emitted map struct — that the heap merge has to resolve. Removing it deletes the test.
//
// The two `wv` bindings are DECLARED unused: constructing the value is the whole test — the
// heap merge happens at the build, and reading the binding back would test a different rung.
// Regression (silent-class-inventory D627).
//
// D624's witness with ONE thing changed — the map's VALUE type. There the field is
// `{[string]: i32}` on both sides, so both ends resolve the same mv shape and the
// slot-equality descent answers. Here the values are `Leaf` and `Leaf2`, themselves layout
// twins: one heap, one vals wrapper, and therefore TWO mv slots emitting byte-identical map
// structs. Slot-index equality cannot see past two indices, so the merge was declined and the
// arm kept a heap of its own — `expected (ref null $type), found (ref $type)`, check rc 0.
//
// THE WIDENING WAS MEASURED IN D624 AND DELIBERATELY NOT TAKEN. Comparing `mvCanonRepOf`
// instead of the slot indices makes this print `7` and costs nothing on the corpus. What
// stopped it was a hazard the corpus cannot show: `mvCanonRepOf` walks `repMapValSlotsTwin`,
// whose cross-table arm re-enters `variantStructHeapTwinAt` — unbounded on a mutually
// recursive map-valued shape, and a new compiler trap is on the veto list.
//
// THE BOUND IS A COINDUCTIVE PAIR STACK, WHICH IS NOT A RE-ENTRANCY GUARD. Deciding two heap
// types are one is equirecursive type equality, and the answer wanted is the GREATEST
// fixpoint: the pair is merged unless assuming it leads to a contradiction. Every finite
// disagreement still returns -1 and unwinds the whole descent, so an assumption survives only
// when nothing in the cycle disagreed. The row's objection to a plain re-entrancy guard —
// that it makes the answer depend on the CALL STACK, so the mint and the pairwise finds
// disagree — does not apply to a pair stack: the assumption is keyed on the very pair being
// decided, so `(vi, si)` determines the answer whatever route reached it. Same argument
// `structFieldCodesEq`'s own pair stack rests on.
//
// The second program is the adversarial shape the row asked for: a self-recursive
// map-valued field WITH a layout twin and a union. It was refused loudly before the fix, so
// the loop was unwitnessed; it now runs, which is what turns the hazard into something
// graded rather than argued.
type Leaf = { n: i32 }
type Leaf2 = { n: i32 }
type Circle = { r: { [string]: Leaf } }
type Dot = { r: { [string]: Leaf2 } }
type Sq = { s: i32 }
type Shape = Circle | Sq
type GW = { g: { [string]: Circle } }

function mkcall(): { [string]: Dot } {
  const cc: { [string]: Dot } = Map()
  cc["k0"] = { r: Map() }
  return cc
}

function outer() {
  const wv: GW = { g: mkcall() }
  print(7)
}
outer()

// The recursion the bound exists for.
type A = { r: { [string]: A } | null }
type A2 = { r: { [string]: A2 } | null }
type Sq2 = { s: i32 }
type Shape2 = A | Sq2

function rec() {
  const wv: A = { r: null }
  print(8)
}
rec()
